1. Install Ubuntu (or any Linux distribution) on your system using a virtual machine tool like VirtualBox, and take a screenshot of your successful login to the Linux terminal.
=>Steps
1. Download the Ubuntu Desktop ISO.
2. Open VMware Workstation/VirtualBox.
3. Create a new virtual machine.
4. Select the Ubuntu ISO as the installation media.
5. Allocate suitable RAM, CPU, and storage.
6. Install Ubuntu and create a user account.
7. After installation, open the Terminal.
8. Log in using the created Linux user accoun

2. Open the Linux terminal and use the commands pwd, ls, and cd to navigate to your Downloads folder, then list all files present in it.<br><br><em><strong>Hint:</strong> Use 'cd ~/Downloads' to move to the Downloads directory.</em>
=>1. Check the current directory
● pwd
2. Move to the Downloads directory
● cd ~/Downloads
3. List the files
● ls
The ls command displays the files and directories present in the current
directory

3. Create a new directory named 'music_playlist' inside your home directory, and inside it, create three empty files named song1.mp3, song2.mp3, and song3.mp3 using a single Linux command.
=>The required directory is music_playlist, and it should contain:
● song1.mp3
● song2.mp3
● song3.mp3
mkdir -p ~/music_playlist touch ~/music_playlist/song1.mp3
~/music_playlist/song2.mp3 ~/music_playlist/song3.mp3
Verify the files
● ls -l ~/music_playlist


4. Change the permissions of the 'music_playlist' directory so that only you (the owner) have read, write, and execute permissions, and no one else has any permissions.<br><br><em><strong>Hint:</strong> Use the chmod command with the correct numeric code.</em>
=>The requirement is:
● Owner: Read, Write, Execute
● Group: No permissions
● Others: No permissions
The correct numeric permission is:
700
Use:
● chmod 700 ~/music_playlist

5. Use the man command in the Linux terminal to find out what the 'grep' command does, and write a one-line summary in your own words.
=>Open the manual page:
● man grep
To search inside the manual, you can type:
● /pattern
One-line summary
● grep searches text for lines that match a specified pattern and
displays the matching lines.
